          "instructions": "Questions 35-38",
          "shared_content_html": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">35. Shark meshing uses nets laid<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> A</strong> along the coastline<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> B</strong> at an angle to the beach<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> C</strong> from the beach to the sea</p><p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">36. Other places that have taken up shark meshing include<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> A</strong> South Africa<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> B</strong> New Zealand<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> C</strong> Tahiti</p><p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">37. The average number of sharks caught in nets each year is<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> A</strong> 15<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> B</strong> 150<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> C</strong> 1,500</p><p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">38. Most sharks are caught in<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> A</strong> spring<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> B</strong> summer<br/><strong><input type=\"checkbox\"/> C</strong> winter</p>",
