      "content_html": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>YES\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 </strong>\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 if the statement agrees with the views of the writer<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>NO</strong> \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 if the statement contradicts the views of the writer<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>NOT GIVEN</strong>\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0</span></p><p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">27. Teachers and students\u2019 personal experiences contribute to the popularity of the learning styles concept.<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">28. Research into learning styles was popular in the 1970s.<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">29. Psychologists found evidence for the idea of learning styles as early as 2004.<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">30. The team of psychologists led by Daniel Willingham found evidence for the idea of learning styles.<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\">31. Students may learn better when they are taught using methods they are not familiar with.</span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>EXPLANATIONS FOR THE FINDINGS</strong></span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">One explanation is that some students might not have a strong enough (32) \u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026. to affect their learning. Another possibility is that students may have preferences about how they learn, but these do not affect their (33) \u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026. A third possibility is that students\u2019 preferences do affect their learning, but only because they have learned less well through other methods in the past.</span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">The most likely explanation is that different ways of learning are useful for learning different things. For example, learning to drive a car involves visual learning, auditory learning and (34) \u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026.</span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">The learning-styles approach is not only unsupported by science, but may actually be (35) \u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026.</span></p><p><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>A</strong> harmful<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>B</strong> ability<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>C</strong> hands-on learning<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>D</strong> learning<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>E</strong> preference<br/>\n</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>F</strong> useful</span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Complete the sentences below. Choose <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 36-40 on your answer sheet.</span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">The idea of learning styles can give students the wrong idea about their level of (36) \u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026. Students who believe that intelligence is (37) \u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026 tend to do better than other students.</span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Students who have been told that they are (38) \u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026 learners might not try so hard to learn by reading or listening.</span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Research has shown that students who use a (39) \u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026.. of learning methods often learn more effectively.</span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span style=\"color: #000000;\">In a 2018 study, 78 percent of students said that they had a particular (40) \u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026\u2026</span></p><p><br/>\n<br/>\n<ins class=\"adsbygoogle\" data-ad-client=\"ca-pub-5700321813195736\" data-ad-format=\"auto\" data-ad-slot=\"7372142965\" data-full-width-responsive=\"true\" style=\"display:block\"></ins><br/> </p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><input bg_collapse_expand=\"6abd7efa3f8ef3074717168\" type=\"hidden\" value=\"6abd7efa3f8ef3074717168\"/><input id=\"bg-show-more-text-6abd7efa3f8ef3074717168\" type=\"hidden\" value=\"Show Answers\"/><input id=\"bg-show-less-text-6abd7efa3f8ef3074717168\" type=\"hidden\" value=\"Hide Answers\"/></p><p style=\"text-align: justify\">1. False<br/>\n2. True<br/>\n3. Not given<br/>\n4. True<br/>\n5. Not given<br/>\n6. Treu<br/>\n7. True<br/>\n8. Medical appointments<br/>\n9. Prescriptions<br/>\n10. Unique number<br/>\n11. Buses<br/>\n12. Ferries<br/>\n13. Trams<br/>\n14. A<br/>\n15. A<br/>\n16. C<br/>\n17. B<br/>\n18. D<br/>\n19. Somatic<br/>\n20. Generations<br/>\n21. Diseases<br/>\n22. Reinserted<br/>\n23. True<br/>\n24. Not given<br/>\n25. Not given<br/>\n26. True<br/>\n27. Yes<br/>\n28. Not given<br/>\n29. No<br/>\n30. No<br/>\n31. Yes<br/>\n32. E<br/>\n33. D<br/>\n34. C<br/>\n35. A<br/>\n36. Intelligence<br/>\n37. Changeable<br/>\n38. Visual<br/>\n39. Mix<br/>\n40. (learning) style</p><p style=\"text-align: justify\"></p>",
